WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.780
YouTube law states that you can't do a video 
about "Fifty Shades of Grey"

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.560
without wearing fuzzy pink handcuffs or some other non-threatening bachelorette party-level signifier of kink

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.240
that inadvertently captures the dissonance between 
the intensity of the marketing around "Fifty Shades of Grey"

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:18.700
as some really taboo-pushing kink
and the actual product.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.680
Anyway, here's a lukewarm defense of the first movie

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:42.260
All right, history lesson:  
"Master of the Universe" is a 2009 to 2011 serial "Twilight" fan fiction

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:48.120
written by Erika Mitchell under the pen name "Snowqueens Icedragon" and collected into two volumes.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.780
In this alternate universe, 
Bella Swan is a university student

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:58.980
who falls into the orbit of vampy (but not actually a vampire) billionaire Edward Cullen after interviewing him for the school paper.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.060
The relationship rapidly and clumsily escalates, with Edward stalking Bella,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.580
kidnapping her, revealing his deep dark secret that he's really into some spanky stuff,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.760
then they go back and forth with Bella being alternately scared and intrigued,

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.280
and repeat for about 700 pages,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.220
with a couple of stalker subplots and a helicopter crash sprinkled into the back half,

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:22.000
and that gets you to the end of Part 1, where Edward asks Bella to marry him,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.760
the final chapter ending on a cliffhanger where an unnamed character swears his revenge on the Cullens

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.920
as he watches them from across the lake.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.760
In Part 2, Bella and Edward get married and accidentally pregnant,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.760
Edward's sister Alice is kidnapped by Bella's former boss (one of the stalkers from the previous volume),

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.440
Bella pays the ransom and shoots the guy in the leg,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.220
Edward is like, "Fine, whatever,"

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.780
their kid is born, the end, I guess...
Happily ever after...

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.080
They move into a really, really big house,
but keep the downtown condo as a dedicated sex den.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.540
On a structural level, "Master of the Universe" is an alternate universe, all human, retelling of "Twilight",

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.320
so it doesn't claim to take place in a contiguous universe with the original novels,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:05.800
and the supernatural elements have been removed,

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.880
while the story itself remains largely the same, 
just adapted for the new gimmick.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:18.180
In this case Bella being a university student, and Edward being a billionaire who likes to beat brunette girls that look like his birth mother.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:27.460
[READING FROM EXCERPT]
"I'm a sadist, Bella. I like to whip little brown-haired girls like you because you all look like the crack whore - my birth mother."

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.060
Still, it follows Twilight's plot across the broad strokes:

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.080
Bella is pushed into a scenario where she meets Edward,

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.800
they have some back-and-forth,

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.640
Edward saves Bella from an accident,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.980
they start a relationship after 
Edward reveals his dark secret,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.260
there's a dinner with Edward's family,

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.920
and the whole thing culminates in a moment where Edward goes too far with his dark secret.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:54.540
As a serial fanfiction, "Master of the Universe" was published first in pieces on fanfiction.net,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:01.140
and starting in early 2010, Mitchell began parallel publishing raunchier versions on her personal site, FiftyShades.com.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.080
Within the scope of fanfiction.net the story was a genuine hit,

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:13.420
attracting an estimated 16,000 to 20,000 readers at a very high level of engagement, with people collectively leaving thousands of comments and reviews,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.080
in addition to talking about it on forums and sharing it on their blogs.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.700
This engagement was further lubricated by Mitchell's aggressive publication speed and her accessibility;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:29.880
chapters were often coming out every two days for several weeks at a stretch, pushing the story to the front page of recently updated stories,

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:36.900
and "Icy", as she was called within the community, was quick to pick fights with negative reviews and heap praise on positive ones.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.220
This type of spiraling engagement 
forms a feedback loop

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.820
where readers see this story with thousands of comments amid stories with dozens,

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:51.740
and feel compelled to be in the know;
to find out what this thing is that everyone is talking about.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:57.260
The novel "Fifty Shades of Grey", written by Erika Mitchell under the pen name E.L. James,

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.620
was published in May 2011 by the Writer's Coffee Shop,

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:05.040
an Australian boutique publisher focused on turning 
fan fiction into commercial literature.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:11.260
It is a lightly edited version of roughly the first half of "Master of the Universe: Part 1" where the names have been changed.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.020
Edward Cullen is now Christian Grey, and Bella Swan is now Anastasia Steele.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.040
When I say 'lightly edited', I do mean lightly,

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:25.200
for example: Christian now spends $43 at Ana's store, rather than the $53 Edward spent at Bella's,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.040
and these ellipses are now commas.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.200
Yes, yes, there's more changes than that;

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:35.600
if you want to go by absolute number, there's a lot of changes, thousands even, once you include punctuation,

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.460
but it's an understatement to even say that the core of the two is the same.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:45.320
The two texts, side-by-side, read almost the same, with really only a minor pass for rewording.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.060
The plot and general structure remains untouched.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:56.260
Due to Mitchell's existing passionate fan base and landing right at the time when e-readers were really breaking into the mainstream,

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:02.560
the e-book of "Fifty Shades" was released to strong initial sales; sending it to the top of the best sellers lists,

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.800
and priming a similar feedback loop,
where more and more people pick it up and start talking about it

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:12.400
in order to be in the know 
about the thing that everyone is talking about.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:20.580
The feedback loop was further compounded by 
the remainder of "Master of the Universe" being published in September 2011 and January 2012

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.100
as "Fifty Shades Darker" and "Fifty Shades Freed", with both books seeing similarly strong sales

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:31.360
in addition to engagement, both positive and negative, on the social cataloging site Goodreads.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:40.100
With multiple erotica books from the same series on the best sellers list, media quickly picked up on it as a curiosity, further boosting its profile.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:47.640
By the end of 2011, the "Fifty Shades" books had sold around 250,000 copies and drawn the attention of major publishers.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.260
After a short bidding war,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:55.560
Vintage - an imprint of Random House, now Penguin Random House - acquired the publication rights in March 2012,

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.100
releasing the entire trilogy as mass-market paperbacks in April 2012,

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:05.640
flooding bookstores around the globe, and cementing "Fifty Shades" as a genuine cultural phenomenon.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:12.160
In May 2013, Universal and Focus Features acquired the film rights for approximately $5 million US,

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.860
and production began on the big-screen adaptation of the trilogy, to be directed by Sam Taylor-Johnson

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.040
starring Dakota Johnson as Ana and Jamie Dornan as Christian,

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.020
with the first film slated for release 
Valentine's Day weekend 2015.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.120
Now the books are...

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:28.210
well...

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:32.310
they're pretty bad, but I want to cut a fine line here.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:36.240
There's a lot of criticism that has come at "Fifty Shades" from a lot of different angles;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.960
some valid and some distinctly less valid.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.480
So for example: 
the prose and the dialogue are pretty abysmal.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:51.810
This is valid criticism. The serial nature of the original publication is something that was never worked out in the editing process,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.260
so plot threads will abruptly end,

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:58.080
the story will get side-tracked in a random aside for several chapters at a time

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.920
that will ultimately be dropped and forgotten,

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:02.480
things are rarely foreshadowed,

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:08.920
and at least one character that is integral to the plot, Linc, is only mentioned in passing like...

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:13.220
four times in the entire series,
and never actually shows up.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:14.700
It needs to be acknowledged,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:18.000
as with its origin as an alternate universe retelling of "Twilight",

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.900
and having seen so few alterations during editing,

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:25.620
"Fifty Shades" falls into some serious gray areas of plagiarism.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:29.700
[DIALOGUE FROM TWILIGHT]
"Beautiful? This is the skin of a killer, Bella."

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.440
I mean "Fifty Shades" has aped "Twilight" 
all the way down to the cover design,

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:37.880
and shortly after Stephenie Meyer scuttled plans for "Midnight Sun",

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.520
a version of "Twilight" told from Edward's point of view,

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:46.040
Mitchell announced her plans to retell "Fifty Shades" from Christian's point of view.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:50.800
While Meyer has refrained from litigating anything about the relationship between "Twilight" and "Fifty Shades",

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:55.000
Mitchell's lawyers have not been so magnanimous and kind,

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:59.300
cracking down heavily on derivative works that capitalize on the popularity of "Fifty Shades",

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.659
describing them as "parasitic".

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:05.680
This is itself an extension of Mitchell's willingness to pick fights with critics,

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.660
down to starting petty arguments with people on Twitter.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:14.880
The story also, via Bella's/Ana's running internal commentary, perpetuates dominant cultural narratives

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:19.520
that pressure women to take responsibility for the abuse that their intimate partners inflict on them,

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:24.740
and much of the interaction between the leads is more than a little hazy in the area of consent

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:28.760
in a way that many critics find to be not merely controversial,

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.740
but disingenuous and even dangerous.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:34.840
Now, without getting too deep into the weeds

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:38.400
I want to acknowledge that fantasy fiction, 
erotic fiction,

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.400
playing with ideas and scenarios that blur the lines of consent or even step right over them,

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:46.740
aren't strictly illegitimate as subject matter.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:51.060
Most of our fiction involves scenarios that we would never want to actually experience,

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:55.840
whether that be getting caught in a gunfight, or trapped at a cabin in the woods with a killer,

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:02.720
and the idea that some people find non-consensual sexual scenarios thrilling is maybe not as weird as it's often painted.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:08.940
Christine Love's visual novel "Ladykiller in a Bind" is a text that delves quite far into scenarios of dubious consent

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:13.020
for the purpose of both titillating 
and challenging the audience.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:17.040
It is an experience that can get rough at times, pun intended.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:22.640
Playing through one scenario left me with a lingering sense of betrayal that took several days to fully process.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:33.120
The game opens, however, with a reminder that fiction is a means for people to explore ideas and scenarios in ways that are safe, or at least safer.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.020
In a video game, you effectively have 
the ultimate safe word.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:39.680
You can exit the program at any time and 
it will stop instantly.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:44.199
It's like the fuzzy pink handcuffs; 
they're not real, they're just toys.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:49.060
They look and behave close enough to real handcuffs, but the key is just for show.

00:09:49.399 --> 00:09:52.440
It's actually less convenient than just using the lever.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:56.708
In most situations, someone wearing them 
can pretty effortlessly get themselves out.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:03.080
They are a form of performative fiction; 
they are the illusion of danger.

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:08.320
They are a safer way to explore the sensation of restraint, of loss of control.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.720
But that isn't to say that they are truly safe.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.340
You can still wear them too tight,
you can still ignore warning signs,

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:20.980
and you can still end up in an awful position that does lasting damage.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:25.460
Similarly, fiction provides us a way to explore ideas and scenarios that would be hazardous,

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:28.640
traumatizing, or both, in real life.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:34.980
Fiction is a way to practice intense emotional states, but that doesn't make fiction harmless.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:39.549
It still impacts us,
 and indeed impacting us is the entire point.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:46.160
This is why critics argue that the subject of fictional non-consent can still be handled more or less ethically,

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.880
an issue that comes down to framing, something that "Fifty Shades" routinely fails at, and fails hard.

00:10:51.889 --> 00:10:57.429
It's not merely that "Master of the Universe" and "Fifty Shades" wander into areas of dubious consent;

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:04.180
It's that Erika Mitchell as a writer doesn't seem to be entirely aware that that is the territory that she's in.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:08.440
In a synergy of failings, Mitchell has responded aggressively to critics raising this point,

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:14.540
including one delightful incident where she angrily tweeted at a rape survivor criticizing the way Christian treats Ana

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:20.060
"No rape in 50 Shades", 
with the attached gif "#READ THE BOOK".

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:25.480
Critics within the BDSM and kink communities have additionally focused on the book's bad and unhealthy

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:30.720
representation of kink dynamics, and the way that it presents kink as something that people get into

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:34.960
only because they have serious unresolved issues with past abuse.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:43.940
These two veins of criticism merge into the synthesis criticism that "Fifty Shades of Grey" does not depict either a healthy or unhealthy kink lifestyle,

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:47.540
but routine domestic abuse being masked as "kink"

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:51.520
with critics pointing to the many ways that Christian works to undermine Ana's mental well-being,

00:11:51.619 --> 00:11:54.729
her sense of stability, and her ability to consent.

00:11:54.729 --> 00:12:01.020
He negotiates their sex contract while plying her with alcohol, isolates her physically and emotionally from friends and family,

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:07.900
belittles her interests, mocks her sexual inexperience, regularly leaves her in tears, makes her financially dependent on his approval,

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:13.360
and constantly reminds her of both his vast wealth and his willingness to use it to monitor and pursue her.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:18.480
In a 2012 interview with "Books In Review", 
Mitchell responded to such criticism with this:

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:43.120
So it is fair to say that Mitchell herself does not see the parallels, believes that she has crafted a fair,

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:49.160
if fantasized version, of a kink lifestyle, and has been openly resentful of critics who insist otherwise.

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:55.880
However, even with all these criticisms, it needs to be acknowledged that a huge chunk of the backlash to "Fifty Shades"

00:12:55.880 --> 00:13:04.100
stemmed from and manifested as rampant misogyny and an assault on the public visibility of female sexuality and sexual agency,

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:06.960
with many, many people using legitimate criticism

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:13.080
as a springboard to mock "mommy porn" and the very idea of women finding anything erotic

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:17.560
if it didn't conform to strict male-centric ideas of feminine sexuality.

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:25.940
In this context, it's not even weird to consider that a certain component of "Fifty Shades"'s success can be attributed to a collective political act of defiance:

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:33.200
women publicly embracing the book, not for its quality or even because it suited their tastes, but because of what its success represents

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:38.640
in a culture that generally sees pornography for women as both setup and punchline.

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:42.640
So, before we start really digging into the meat of "Fifty Shades"'s failings,

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:46.380
I do want to take a moment to talk about what resonated with audiences.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:52.980
The books aren't good, but it's disingenuous to insist that no one could then engage with them authentically.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:56.820
It has clearly struck a chord with its core audience,

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:01.860
and developed a loyal fan base that likes it for what it is: in whole or in part.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:07.460
The first unavoidable connection is as fan fiction, which is where the earliest fans came on board

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:12.600
They weren't reading an original construct,
but an explicit derivative

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:16.740
One of the advantages, and indeed one of the foundational appeals of fan fiction,

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:24.740
is the ability to shortcut to "the good stuff", because so much about the characters and the world is able to be presupposed

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:31.080
This also (as a side note), often leaves fan fiction with thumb prints all over it, as the opening chapters of an alternate universe story

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:38.100
are going to be largely devoted to getting the audience caught up on the deviations from the core they are already familiar with

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:42.880
This is why Fifty Shades, for example, calls explicit attention to Ana getting drunk for the first time

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:47.700
It's an explicit deviation in Bella's character
 as written in Twilight

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:56.420
But there's this appeal to seeing characters that the audience has already formed a parasocial bond with in synthesized scenarios and novel circumstances

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:59.700
This isn't even all that odd *outside* the realm
of fan fiction

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:05.500
DC Comics over the years has imagined Batman as a vampire, as a pirate, as a medieval knight,

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:14.200
and as an 18th century detective chasing Jack the Ripper - just to name a few of the literally dozens of alternate universe Batmen that are out there...

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:20.940
At a certain point, characters cease to require their context to be whole, and in fact become their own context,

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:28.280
and subsequently the fun comes from taking them and moving them around; playing with them in a variety of different styles and scenarios...

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:35.380
In this way, fan fiction is the literary evolution of childhood play: taking disparate toys and bringing them together;

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:38.380
an almost instinctive exercise in creative synthesis

00:15:38.399 --> 00:15:41.369
It is not a mystery that fanfiction appeals to so many,

00:15:41.709 --> 00:15:48.659
because it is naturally what stories pursue: the meeting point between the familiar and the unexpected.

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:50.940
Moving beyond the fanfiction,

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:54.840
many fans enjoy indulging in economic fantasies;

00:15:54.840 --> 00:16:03.300
the thrill of absurd wealth and privilege: the story about an ordinary girl plucked out of obscurity by an incomprehensibly rich dude

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:06.760
who is able to give her virtually anything that she wants.

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:11.060
Indeed, elements that many critics have found odd, 
or infuriating,

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:14.280
are to the invested reader, part of the appeal

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:18.860
Ana rejects Christian's gifts, 
which he then imposes on her

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:24.460
This allows the reader to indulge the dual fantasy of maintaining a sense of social propriety

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:28.760
(the self-perception of being the person who is humble, down-to-earth, and proper),

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:35.060
while still enjoying all the material benefits of dating a man with more money than an individual could ever spend

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:39.960
Also, we shouldn't leave it merely implied: 
many people enjoy the pornography

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:43.320
They find the sexual dynamics and writing authentically erotic.

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:48.500
That may seem unbelievable because the writing is so often just painful,

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:54.240
but what do we benefit critically for pretending that no one could sincerely enjoy the material?

00:16:54.420 --> 00:17:03.640
Conversely, simply because people enjoy it authentically, doesn't mean that criticism of the material (either in terms of technique or subject matter) become invalid.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:08.940
Critical readings of Fifty Shades that document patterns of abuse: how Christian isn't merely dominant,

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:14.380
domineering, or even just an asshole - but works to 
tear down Ana, coerce her,

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:17.920
intentionally confound her ability to consent, and isolate her socially:

00:17:17.949 --> 00:17:24.509
These are still valid and important readings, even if there's an audience that finds it sexually stimulating

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:26.589
So with all that in mind

00:17:26.589 --> 00:17:28.760
What's wrong with Fifty Shades of Grey?

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:30.800
Well, a lot..

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:37.240
A big thing that I want to talk about, because it's going to be the most relevant later, is the legacy of serialization

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:40.040
This is an issue that compounds as the books go on

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.740
but it's significant pretty much right out of the gate.

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:47.780
In particular, the serial fanfiction method tends towards a sort of public performance

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:50.600
A writer might publish a short dead end chapter,

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:56.160
purely because they haven't posted anything in a few weeks and their fans are wondering if the fic is dead

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:58.560
Ideas might catch the author's attention

00:17:58.560 --> 00:17:59.580
but after a few weeks

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:03.220
they decide it just isn't going the direction they or the audience want

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:04.860
and so the plot thread is abandoned.

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:11.180
Because the nature of serial fiction is to always keep pushing forward, and edits are only made chapter-by-chapter,

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:14.400
these problems then persist into further derivatives.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:16.620
For Fifty Shades of Grey in specific,

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:21.240
these mostly manifest as a lot of cyclical repeating scenes in the middle of the book,

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:28.000
petty arguments and conflicts that happen often enough that it's hard to remember which specific one was in which specific chapter,

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:33.140
and numerous side stories that last a chapter or two before abruptly ending.

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:40.580
Now ideally, these are exactly the kind of issues that an editor would address in adapting a fan fiction from forum thread to published novel

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:47.660
and this is as good a time as any to mention that Erica Mitchell is, by all accounts, absolutely awful to work with.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:53.680
Reportedly, the first editor she was assigned at the Writers' Coffee Shop, tasked with turning Master of the Universe into Fifty Shades

00:18:53.680 --> 00:19:00.320
was removed from the project after Mitchell complained that she was trying to make too many changes to the structure of the books

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:04.500
That kind of behavior is going to come up again when we're talking about the movies and

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:08.960
she's spending an hour on set 
screaming at the director over changes to dialogue

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:12.620
Speaking of the dialogue. It's, uh...

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:14.280
...it's not good

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:24.200
I'm going to lump Ana's internal monologue in with this, because the words characters say aloud and the things Ana thinks to herself

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.360
Tend to play off each other in...

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:28.960
...the worst way.

00:19:28.960 --> 00:20:24.440
[NARRATOR reads text]

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:28.540
Mitchell also has an awkward habit of juxtaposing erotic imagery

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:31.200
with infantilizing internal commentary from Anastasia

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:36.580
Ana is seemingly perpetually confused by the world around her and naive to the point of farce,

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:39.860
a thread that persists all the way 
to the very end of the trilogy.

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:46.120
Lastly, Mitchell doesn't have a deft touch when it comes to managing the sexual danger

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:55.040
Again, because she is, or at least publicly performs, being completely unaware and in denial that her writing steers into areas of dubious consent

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:58.080
There are many, many moments where Ana

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:01.820
is legitimately afraid of what Christian might do to her.

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:04.400
to quote romance author Jenny Trout on the issue:

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:23.820
[reads quote]

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:27.300
This is for many readers a source of much of their frustration

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:34.320
The book doesn't manage to balance sexy danger, but it also refuses to embrace the danger danger that it creates

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:41.249
There is a potentially legitimately decent erotic thriller or even erotic horror somewhere inside the subject matter

00:21:41.249 --> 00:21:46.500
but Erika Mitchell seems convinced that living in constant fear of your sexual partner's temper

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.780
is just what a kinky sex life looks like

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:53.320
And...yeah: I think that's enough context

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:56.880
to start talking about the movie

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:06.800
"Fifty Shades of Grey" is a 2015 film written by Kelly Marcel and directed by Sam Taylor-Johnson

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:09.960
Based on the 2011 novel of the same name

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:18.220
In this project, the two women were given the task of wrangling a trashy BDSM erotica novel, full of clumsy plot, abusive threadbare characters, and awful dialogue

00:22:18.409 --> 00:22:19.909
into a sellable movie

00:22:19.909 --> 00:22:26.160
So I'm gonna start this off with the admission
 that I...actually kind of like the first Fifty Shades movie

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:28.760
not because it's any good, but because there's a certain

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:30.700
Creative Verve to it

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:34.560
Like the filmmakers were actually trying 
really hard to capitalize

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:42.640
creatively on this momentary zeitgeist, rather than simply cashing in and riding the money train through three basically guaranteed jobs

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:46.220
There's a dynamic around the first film that I found compelling

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:53.400
particularly how it was cast in many circles as 'the worst film of the year', when really it was just

00:22:53.560 --> 00:22:54.840
fairly middling...

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:57.160
As someone who has read the source material

00:22:57.240 --> 00:23:03.280
I was largely entertained by mentally cataloging all the points where the film had managed to improve on the source material

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:09.900
Coming frustratingly close to making it actually kind of good (or at least trashy fun)

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:13.059
If I took bits and pieces, 
and only showed you the best clips

00:23:13.059 --> 00:23:18.339
I could probably put together a convincing argument that this is actually a really good film

00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:24.200
Director Sam Taylor-Johnson and screenwriter Kelly Marcel both publicly clashed with Mitchell over the course of the project

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:29.740
and in both cases the conflict revolved principally around changes to the source material

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:34.560
This is ironic because the source material is easily the worst thing about the movie.

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:40.680
This is also the thing where... well... I mean  
- the whole thing was ultimately doomed

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:45.700
In the deal with Universal and Focus, Mitchell retained a massive amount of creative control

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:50.380
with something nearly approaching total veto power over any creative decision

00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:54.780
...and well, if her books are any indication

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:57.040
creativity isn't her strong suit...

00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:01.300
Still, the film diverges subtly, 
but substantially from the book

00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:04.540
The middle of the story has been cleaned up 
and more or less sorted out

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:10.740
Scenes have been merged, deleted, had their locations changed  - lots of small adaptational stuff...

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:14.880
A huge change - Ana's inner monologue has been dropped entirely

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:19.820
While she is still the point of view character, with the camera rarely cutting away to scenes that she's not in,

00:24:19.820 --> 00:24:21.780
she is no longer the narrator

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:30.160
This has substantial and far-reaching impacts on both the general tone and reading of the story, but particularly on Ana's character

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:38.220
NARRATION [Ana's inner monologue from the book]: "Double crap! Me and my 2 left feet..."

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:40.360
[movie dialogue - Christian]: 
"Miss Kavanagh?"

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:42.360
NARRATION [Ana's inner monologue from the book]: "Holy cow! He's so young!"

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:44.080
[movie dialogue - Christian]: 
"You alright?"

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:47.300
NARRATION [Ana's inner monologue from the book]: 
"If this guy's over 30, then I'm a monkey's uncle ..."

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:49.040
[movie dialogue - Christian]: "Christian Grey"

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:55.500
NARRATION [Ana's inner monologue from the book]: 
"Holy crap! What the hell is he doing here?"

00:24:55.500 --> 00:25:02.520
NARRATION [Ana's inner monologue from the book]: 
"Looking all outdoorsy with his tousled hair and in his cream chunky knit sweater, jeans, and walking boots..."

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:04.840
[movie dialogue - Christian]: 
"I thought it was you."

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:07.330
[movie dialogue - Christian]: 
"What a pleasant surprise, Miss Steele"

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:09.540
[movie dialogue - Ana]: 
"...just Ana"

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:13.400
[movie dialogue - Christian]: 
"I'm not gonna touch you. 
Not until I have your written consent."

00:25:13.400 --> 00:25:14.660
NARRATION [Ana's inner monologue from the book]: "What does that mean?!"

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:21.400
NARRATION [Ana's inner monologue from the book]: Does he white slave small children to some godforsaken part of the planet? Is he part of some underworld crime syndicate?"

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:29.940
NARRATION [Ana's inner monologue from the book]: 
"It would explain why he's so rich. Is he deeply religious? Is he impotent? Surely not. He could prove that to me right now..."

00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:36.900
Ana is generally made more competent and proactive, simply by excluding all the times in the book where something will happen

00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:43.480
and her inner monologue will comment on how she doesn't understand what's going on or what people are talking about

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:49.260
Movie!Ana is a lot more confident, funnier, emotionally aware, and less of a swooning catastrophe

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:55.160
And the movie cuts out the dozen or so times that small interactions with Christian leave her a sobbing wreck

00:25:55.360 --> 00:26:02.040
She also doesn't complain endlessly in her head about her friends and is quicker to put her foot down when Christian gets out of line

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:08.520
It is left to the audience to extrapolate from Dakota Johnson's acting exactly how comfortable and safe

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:11.560
they think Ana is in any given scene

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:14.800
As a further consequence of moving the point of view out of Ana's head

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:20.560
it dramatically alters the abusive elements of the book, and this in turn ties into changes to Christian

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:25.620
who, as a character, has been softened to be a bit more natural and less volatile.

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:32.620
He doesn't scream at people. He doesn't berate Ana constantly for her eating habits. He doesn't talk constantly about wanting to possess her.

00:26:32.620 --> 00:26:36.540
He's just a...bad, kind of...needy boyfriend.

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:41.240
That's still not good, but it means there's at least something likable about the Movie!Christian,

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:46.660
where Book!Christian is cruel, capricious, 
and constantly on the edge of violence

00:26:46.660 --> 00:27:17.440
[reading from the novel]

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:23.180
And this is a good point to mention that it's kind of okay if Christian is a garbage person; .

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:30.280
Like, from a narrative standpoint: it's not bad that Christian is bad -  that he's a bad person or a bad boyfriend

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:33.529
It's a story; it's drama -  we want conflict

00:27:33.529 --> 00:27:36.200
We want some volatility and excitement,

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:43.100
and even a level of emotional intensity that would be exhausting in real life, but is fun to pretend with for a few hours

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:47.780
Him being a bad boyfriend, a poor communicator, emotionally distant;

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:54.880
these give the characters things to conflict over, things for them to clash against  - generating tension, drama, and story.

00:27:54.880 --> 00:28:00.840
To take an example from later in the film: Christian spanks Ana in a way that the film plays as playful

00:28:00.860 --> 00:28:04.480
There's a lot of good tension in the scene with Ana's conflicting desires.

00:28:04.480 --> 00:28:10.200
And yeah:  Dakota Johnson has played the scene as Ana realizing that she kinda likes it

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:12.760
And then Christian up and leaves

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:17.260
and Ana, being abandoned, 
starts to break down from the intensity of it.

00:28:17.260 --> 00:28:19.160
This is actually really good drama

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:22.020
It's presented as not any one thing

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:26.960
but the aggregate combination of the intensity and Christian's emotional unavailability

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:33.560
It is left to the viewer to decide just how far they think Ana thinks Christian has gone...

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:42.520
What has been removed are the pages of internal monologue where Ana feels like she's been assaulted, but rationalizes it away because of how much she loves Christian

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:56.840
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:28:56.840 --> 00:29:00.640
This is how Book!Ana consistently 
frames the encounter

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:07.360
She doesn't process it as play, but as abuse. And Christian dismisses her concerns out of hand...

00:29:07.520 --> 00:29:39.800
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:44.160
Again, this isn't (from a story perspective) 
strictly bad in isolation

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:46.220
It is a source of conflict

00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:52.660
But the real world implications are troubling and the conflict is never confronted or properly resolved

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:59.760
Christian just tells Ana to get over it; that if she really loved him, she'd be a good submissive and just deal with it

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:04.560
And that friction never becomes part of 
Book!Ana's motivations

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:12.920
Moving on: the entire opening of the film is a great case study in the difference in skill and craft between the original and the filmmakers

00:30:12.920 --> 00:30:16.780
The opening montage is actually really good.

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:20.659
Lots of good leading lines. Lots of well juxtaposed shots and parallels

00:30:20.659 --> 00:30:24.439
It sets the color palette, the musical tone,  the visual pace.

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:27.080
It's really solid filmmaking fundamentals

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:32.160
This shot of Ana looking up at Grey House; where it's all slanted because it's putting her off-kilter

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:37.820
Is great judicious use of a Dutch angle, that also helps fit a very tall building into a narrow frame

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:43.960
Using visuals to contrast Ana and Christian is a much better introduction to the two characters than they get in the book

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:46.740
where they don't...even...really...have one?

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:54.180
because they're clearly Bella and Edward 
(who you already know, because you - dear fanfiction reader - have read all of Twilight...twice)

00:30:54.180 --> 00:31:00.520
[dialogue from the novel - Christian]
"First: I don't make love. I f@ck. Hard."

00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:07.280
A significant alteration to the interview, is injecting a bit of awareness of the lameness of the interview as written in the book

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:13.880
Where Ana asks a series of banal and low-key insulting questions that Christian just answers one-after-the-other

00:31:13.880 --> 00:31:18.620
There's a bit of verbal sparring, but it's overwhelmingly the kind of utilitarian information-dump

00:31:18.620 --> 00:31:23.520
that serves to get the fanfiction reader up to speed on the new rules of the story's universe

00:31:24.060 --> 00:31:30.800
The filmmakers wisely discard most of this and plant two moments in to acknowledge that the interview... [laughingly] kinda sucks

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:35.100
[movie dialogue - Ana]
(awkwardly) "Uh. You are very young to have amassed such an empire; to what do..."

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:36.920
[movie dialogue - Christian]
(breaks in; bored) "To what do I owe my success?

00:31:36.920 --> 00:31:39.000
[movie dialogue - Ana]
(chagrined) "...Yep."

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:43.500
But then unfortunately, the source material 
comes *crashing* back in...

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:46.120
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Are you gay?"

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:48.960
Alright, okay. Okay. Let's pause for a bit...

00:31:48.980 --> 00:31:54.620
So, this is as good a time as any to mention the shades of homophobia and misogyny

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:57.940
that lay over Master of the Universe 
and all subsequent derivatives...

00:31:57.940 --> 00:32:01.320
In the books, given that the reader 
is privy to all of Ana's thoughts,

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:10.180
it becomes obvious - almost comical -  how almost every man and woman that Ana comes across is evaluated as a possible sexual competitor

00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:11.860
Particularly relevant to this scene:

00:32:11.860 --> 00:32:21.780
Christian staffs his office exclusively with hot blonde women in tight skirts and blazers, and Ana starts to refer to them throughout the books as "the Stepford blondes"

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:25.860
Ana's blonde roommate, Kate, also falls into a relationship with a billionaire;

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:32.080
starting a fling with Christian's brother Elliot that also evolves over the course of the books into marriage and babies,

00:32:32.080 --> 00:32:39.520
but Kate is subject to endless internal criticism for doing...well, pretty much exactly what Ana is doing

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:49.120
[reading from the novel]

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:51.340
...Then there's the gay thing.

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:52.820
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Are you gay?"

00:32:52.820 --> 00:32:59.760
There's a character (a masseuse), dropped from the movies, that the books go to comical lengths to point out that he's gay

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:06.960
Totally gay. Super gay. Definitely gay. And thus allowed to be around Ana when Christian isn't there...

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:11.160
then of course there's this; which somehow made it all the way through the adaptation process

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:14.260
including the even stranger companion line from later,

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:21.720
where Ana spreads the rumor to undercut two of her classmates that she overhears talking about how cute her secret boyfriend is

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:24.440
[movie dialogue - classmate 1]
"He is *so* hot."

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:27.240
[movie dialogue - classmate 2]
"Oh gosh! He so is."

00:33:28.900 --> 00:33:32.080
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"I heard he's gay."

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:37.660
I'm actually at a complete loss as to why that's there...

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:44.140
However, bad is this question may be; it does lead into a great example of how much better the film is than the novel

00:33:44.140 --> 00:33:47.520
In the book, Ana and Christian are sitting on a large couch for the entire interview

00:33:47.530 --> 00:33:54.219
they just sit down and talk and talk, and eventually the time for the interview runs out when one of the blondes comes in

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:58.340
and tells him his next meeting is almost ready to start, but Christian cancels it.

00:33:58.340 --> 00:34:01.700
The lead-in to Christian canceling the meeting

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:05.480
is Ana telling Christian that Kate is sick.

00:34:05.480 --> 00:34:35.900
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:40.840
In contrast, here's how the movie handles the same transition point in the conversation

00:34:40.880 --> 00:34:42.880
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"What about you?"

00:34:45.680 --> 00:34:48.280
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"Why don't you ask me something 
that *you* want to know."

00:34:52.720 --> 00:34:56.400
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Earlier, you said that there are some people who know you well..."

00:34:59.020 --> 00:35:02.060
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Why do I get the feeling that that is not true?"

00:35:05.060 --> 00:35:06.980
[movie dialogue - Blonde #2]
"Mr. Grey," your next meeting is in the conference room..."

00:35:06.980 --> 00:35:08.980
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"Cancel it, please. We're not finished here."

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:09.940
[movie dialogue - Blonde #2]
"Yes, sir."

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:17.579
So first of all, we're working with visual levels: Christian spends most of the scenes standing over Ana; a straightforward power position.

00:35:17.579 --> 00:35:22.889
But with the turning point, he moves to the chair; brings himself down to Ana's level

00:35:22.889 --> 00:35:25.100
So we have some visual power play

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:30.780
Second, rather than leading into Christian canceling the meeting with a banal point about Kate being sick

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:34.860
It's a pointed question from Ana that is actually disarming

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:39.300
this question, paraphrased: "Why do I get the feeling no one knows the real you?",

00:35:39.869 --> 00:35:43.229
piques his interest; it creates an honest-to-god emotional exchange

00:35:43.980 --> 00:35:51.000
Christian invites Ana to test him; even disarms himself a little as part of his game, and she cuts right through his facade

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:57.040
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"I just wonder if perhaps your heart might be a bit
bigger than you want to let on?"

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:04.220
But this question also makes Ana into a far more emotionally intelligent character than she is in the novels

00:36:04.220 --> 00:36:08.180
Movie!Anastasia sees right through most of Christian's games

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:11.220
Where the novel version of this exchange is predatory,

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:14.380
(Christian manipulating the largely oblivious Ana)

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:20.660
the film version has Christian attempting to toy with Ana and being caught off guard by her response

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:26.440
This is part of a pattern in the film where Ana has been injected with a lot of much-needed narrative agency

00:36:28.760 --> 00:36:31.100
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"He asked me to go for coffee afterwards."

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:37.260
This one line, for example, actually represents a huge change to Ana's character versus the novels.

00:36:37.360 --> 00:36:39.700
The scene is from chapter 3 of the book

00:36:39.740 --> 00:36:44.280
and in the book, the whole process of
negotiating going for coffee,

00:36:44.300 --> 00:36:50.860
takes *a page and a half* of arguing the logistics of who is going to drive José and his assistant home,

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:54.960
because Ana doesn't actually want to go? or she's not sure if she wants to?

00:36:54.960 --> 00:36:58.320
and maybe she wants to? but she kind of doesn't?

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:43.140
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:37:44.960 --> 00:37:47.580
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"He asked me to go for coffee afterwards."

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:51.880
The filmmakers have put in effort 
to create *actual* escalation

00:37:51.880 --> 00:37:58.060
this small change: having Ana *actually* 
want to go for coffee

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:03.120
at least kinda makes things look like some hot-and-cold romantic tension.

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:08.800
The thing with the books, is that Ana constantly 
(and I do mean do mean CONSTANTLY)

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:15.880
mentally beats the crap out of herself for the idea of liking Christian and having any sort of sexual ideas.

00:38:15.880 --> 00:38:20.880
She is perpetually on the verge 
of a COMPLETE meltdown

00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:22.380
Like, at the end of the coffee scene,

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:26.840
which the filmmakers have mercifully reworked into something resembling human dialog,

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:31.020
Ana is almost hit by a cyclist, 
but is pulled out of the way by Christian

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:32.880
and then they look into each other's eyes,

00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:36.860
and he tells her she should 'stay away' from him because they're no good for each other

00:38:36.860 --> 00:38:38.260
In the film, that's it.

00:38:38.260 --> 00:38:43.360
Ana looks a bit sad because, I mean: she's just being pretty clearly rejected,

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:45.860
but she walks away and goes about her life

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:49.080
and we skip to later in an otherwise normal week...

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:58.080
In the book, she goes into the parking garage and has a complete sobbing meltdown that leaves her curled up in the fetal position on the concrete

00:38:58.180 --> 00:39:14.940
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:21.660
Adding this stability to the character, leads to some of the film's best moments and some legitimately fun and funny lines

00:39:21.660 --> 00:39:28.220
Like: Ana and her friends go out drinking to celebrate the end of their exams, and while standing in line for the washroom, Ana drunk-dials Christian.

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:34.140
While on the phone, she quite accurately mocks Christian's behavior up to that point

00:39:34.380 --> 00:39:36.740
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"Listen to me: I want you to go home right now."

00:39:36.740 --> 00:39:38.620
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"You're so bossy!"

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:42.420
[movie dialogue - Ana]
(imitating Christian) "Ana, let's go for a coffee. 
(deep, sultry voice) Ana, let's go for coffee."

00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:46.660
[movie dialogue - Ana]
(imitating Christian) "No. Stay away from me, Ana. 
I don't want you."

00:39:46.660 --> 00:39:49.800
[movie dialogue - Ana]
(imitating Christian) Get away. 
Come here! Come here! Go away."

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:50.980
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"I said, tell me where you are."

00:39:50.980 --> 00:39:53.260
It's great. That's hilarious.

00:39:53.320 --> 00:40:00.320
This of course works, because Movie!Ana is actually able to more or less accurately read his emotional state

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:03.520
where Book!Ana is confused by everything...

00:40:03.520 --> 00:40:06.700
Now up to this point, 
(we're about 22 minutes into the movie),

00:40:06.700 --> 00:40:14.400
the filmmakers have done an admirable job of massaging the story into something with actual pacing and maybe a little less nonsense,

00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:18.520
within the constraint of not being allowed to alter any plot points

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:24.040
Unfortunately, this scene is when they run up against that as an inescapable problem

00:40:24.100 --> 00:40:28.420
it's the big jump in the plot that 
needed the most work to smooth it over

00:40:28.480 --> 00:40:31.100
and they weren't allowed to touch it...

00:40:31.100 --> 00:40:34.780
So, going off the broad template for these types of stories

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:39.520
we have our hot-and-cold intro, where the two characters circle each other for a bit

00:40:39.520 --> 00:40:45.220
Then the first big break, where they fall apart, and then something that brings them crashing back into each other

00:40:45.400 --> 00:40:49.400
Gwen Hayes, in her book, "Romancing the Beat: 
how to write kissing books"

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:53.940
calls this moment "adhesion", because it doesn't just bring the characters back together,

00:40:54.020 --> 00:40:56.800
but is the point that they get *stuck*.

00:40:56.800 --> 00:41:01.140
That last point can be really hard to come up with in a satisfying way

00:41:01.140 --> 00:41:04.660
Especially when the characters are, as is the case here,

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:08.560
from two radically different worlds that don't really intersect.

00:41:08.740 --> 00:41:13.680
How do you get the billionaire and the University student back together?

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:17.559
How do you get them to stay together? It's challenging...

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:23.349
So, being in a situation where they were forbidden from changing the plot point, the filmmakers had a choice:

00:41:23.860 --> 00:41:27.500
Do we keep the detail about Christian hacking her phone or not?

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:44.260
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:41:44.380 --> 00:41:46.460
Yeah, maybe a good skip there...

00:41:46.460 --> 00:41:51.000
The scene still has problems: like Elliot 
just sort of being invented out of nowhere,

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:54.640
Christian and Elliot being inside the bar already, 
stuff like that...

00:41:54.640 --> 00:41:56.140
A lot changes very quickly...

00:41:56.240 --> 00:42:02.740
This is somewhat mitigated in the movie, because the cut from standing in line for the washroom, to leaving the bar, is a bit more ambiguous

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:06.300
(where the book follows Ana through every step in between)

00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:11.960
and I'm pretty sure Christian and Elliot get to the bar faster than they should have been able to get to the hotel's parking garage...

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:13.580
And since we're at the bar,

00:42:13.580 --> 00:42:15.940
Okay: we should talk about José.

00:42:15.940 --> 00:42:20.820
So the problem with José, is a problem that goes back to the very beginning

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:26.960
and I'm not talking about the low-key racism, the "Dios mios", or the fact that José tries to molest Ana

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:30.520
though, you know: make note of all of those...

00:42:30.520 --> 00:42:34.060
No, what we're talking about is the narrative utility of José

00:42:34.060 --> 00:42:38.200
and how he fits (or more often fails to fit) into the story.

00:42:38.200 --> 00:42:39.940
Here's what José does in the books:

00:42:39.940 --> 00:42:44.200
He's Ana and Kate's friend, though he's an engineering major and not an English major

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:47.400
He does photography, though mostly of architecture

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:52.940
but gets press-ganged into doing Christian's head shots for Kate's article, because her normal photographer isn't available.

00:42:52.940 --> 00:43:00.860
After exams are done, he goes out drinking with Kate and Ana, tries to get Ana super drunk, then gropes her in the parking lot before Christian intervenes

00:43:00.900 --> 00:43:09.620
Marcell and Taylor Johnson had the right idea that at this point, José's narrative utility (if not his friendship with Ana) is done.

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:12.320
And he more or less disappears for the rest of the movie...

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:20.800
In the book, he continues to float around in the background and Ana's like: 'oh, what's a little attempted date-rape between friends?'

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:22.090
'Just don't do it again!'

00:43:22.090 --> 00:43:27.740
and he's always sort of there, as though he's going to form part of a love triangle that never happens,

00:43:27.740 --> 00:43:33.340
because he's Jacob Black; and you can't just cut him out of your fanfiction or Team Jacob will revolt.

00:43:33.340 --> 00:43:35.340
It's a little weird...

00:43:35.700 --> 00:43:37.280
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"It's just behind this door."

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:39.260
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"What is?"

00:43:39.260 --> 00:43:40.520
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"My play room."

00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:43.460
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Like your Xbox and stuff?"

00:43:43.560 --> 00:43:49.320
The dialogue in the playroom is lifted straight out of the book, so of course it's all...awful."

00:43:49.380 --> 00:43:50.880
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"I'm a dominant."

00:43:50.880 --> 00:43:52.880
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"What does that mean?"

00:43:52.880 --> 00:43:55.320
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"It means I want you to willingly 
surrender yourself to me."

00:43:55.320 --> 00:43:57.960
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Whyyyy would I do that?"

00:43:57.960 --> 00:43:59.740
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"To please me."

00:44:00.100 --> 00:44:03.720
All the scenes in the playroom are the scenes that, according to Sam Taylor-Johnson,

00:44:03.780 --> 00:44:06.140
Mitchell was most protective of.

00:44:06.140 --> 00:44:09.620
Christian does an awful job at 
pitching the whole situation,

00:44:09.740 --> 00:44:15.540
which kiiiiiinda makes sense given what his actual damage is,

00:44:15.580 --> 00:44:23.880
but the problem is that we're not going to learn that he's actually really bad at the whole Dom thing until really far into the next movie

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:28.460
Like, okay: it isn't actually acknowledged until

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:32.200
...842 pages into the series

00:44:32.860 --> 00:44:41.400
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:44:41.660 --> 00:44:46.380
Even then, that subject is going to be more or less compartmentalized to a single scene

00:44:46.400 --> 00:44:49.860
It's an element that's never confronted, 
so much as sidestepped,

00:44:49.880 --> 00:44:57.080
by having the characters never actually engage in any meaningful S/D interactions beyond Ana's lightweight brat routine,

00:44:57.080 --> 00:45:01.060
the outcome of which is mostly light bondage and a bit of spanking...

00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:05.900
It's also undercut by the whole thing where Christian insists that he doesn't do dates,

00:45:05.900 --> 00:45:10.440
has never slept in the same bed as someone else, doesn't do romance and flowers and lovemaking,

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:15.360
but he does: he took her out for coffee...

00:45:15.460 --> 00:45:16.739
That's a date.

00:45:16.740 --> 00:45:24.280
At least they follow up this first playroom scene with another substantial change for the better, when Ana tells Christian that she's still a virgin

00:45:27.280 --> 00:45:29.860
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Because I have...I haven't..."

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:31.960
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"You're still a virgin?"

00:45:32.060 --> 00:45:39.720
Now, this plot point is retained from the book and it still feels kind of fetishistic and is a bit off-putting for that,

00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:42.420
but how its responded to is substantially better.

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:48.940
In the book, Christian stalks around the room, running his hands through his hair, yelling and growling, and castigating Ana.

00:45:59.380 --> 00:46:38.380
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:46:38.640 --> 00:46:43.140
Marcel and Taylor-Johnson on the other hand, 
lay on the mushy romance

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:47.720
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"Where have you been?"

00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:50.320
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Waiting."

00:46:51.640 --> 00:46:53.640
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"Men must throw themselves at you."

00:46:56.080 --> 00:46:58.080
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Never one I've wanted."

00:46:58.940 --> 00:47:04.600
This does, however, just further detail that basic flaw in the telling of Christian's character

00:47:04.660 --> 00:47:11.880
Christian has spent multiple scenes telling Ana all about how he doesn't do romance, doesn't do flowers, doesn't do dates...

00:47:12.220 --> 00:47:15.060
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"First, I don't make love."

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:19.220
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"I f@ck. Hard."

00:47:19.440 --> 00:47:25.580
But at a pin drop, he just decides some old-fashioned lovemaking is in order, so...

00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:31.900
Yeah... I try not to get in the habit of suggesting fixes, but it's really hard not to do that here

00:47:31.940 --> 00:47:38.820
Just have Ana be the one to escalate once they start kissing. I mean, that's the whole conflict here, isn't it?

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:43.280
Ana's moony-eyed romance vs 
Christian's business-like dominance?

00:47:43.280 --> 00:47:45.220
Maybe have a bit more push and pull with that;

00:47:45.220 --> 00:47:49.300
he gets her to try out some spanky stuff and she finds out she likes it,

00:47:49.300 --> 00:47:53.739
he opens up a bit to flowers and romance and finds out it's not so bad either...

00:47:53.900 --> 00:47:57.160
That kinda is the big emotional arc here...

00:47:57.160 --> 00:48:02.280
And it is *eventually* acknowledged when Christian agrees to a regular date night once a week

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:04.140
(that, for the record, never happens)

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:11.180
But maybe introduce that idea a bit earlier than, I don't know, 260 pages into the book ?

00:48:11.180 --> 00:48:16.720
But that's really an unfair comparison, because again, in the book version of this scene,

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:25.060
Christian is stalking the room and yelling at Ana, and talks at length about how he's just having sex with her to rectify her virginity

00:48:25.060 --> 00:48:30.860
Which he sees as a problem...sooooooooo...yeah.

00:48:32.440 --> 00:48:35.320
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"I've never taken anyone in the helicopter."

00:48:36.100 --> 00:48:39.420
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"I've never had sex in my own bed."

00:48:41.200 --> 00:48:43.620
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"I've never slept next to anyone."

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:47.830
When they're walking out in the woods on the way back to Portland, this happens again

00:48:47.830 --> 00:48:54.340
where Christian tells Ana (and us) all the things he's done with Ana, that he's never done with anyone else

00:48:54.340 --> 00:48:59.840
Like taking them in the helicopter, having sex in his sleeping bed instead of his designated sex bed,

00:48:59.840 --> 00:49:04.020
having sex that was outside the frame of a submission and domination relationship...

00:49:04.040 --> 00:49:10.330
But the impact of that is undercut by the fact that there hasn't really been any conflict there

00:49:10.340 --> 00:49:15.430
There's no friction for Christian. He's not unsure of himself. He's not lost.

00:49:15.430 --> 00:49:20.000
He enjoys it well enough, 
so it's not a character growth moment.

00:49:20.000 --> 00:49:26.500
Really, he's just describing the fact that yesterday he experienced an incredibly banal first...

00:49:26.620 --> 00:49:32.640
[mock alternate dialogue - Christian]
"Ana, before yesterday, I'd never had Boo Berry before. We always got Corn Pops..."

00:49:32.640 --> 00:49:38.620
[mock alternate dialogue - Christian]
"And when you put rooster sauce on the eggs; I'd never done that before either. They were ok..."

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:44.300
So what this scene ends up doing, which I think almost works if you put this first film in a bottle,

00:49:44.300 --> 00:49:49.480
is it emphasizes that Christian's reticence to compromise is pretty unreasonable

00:49:49.480 --> 00:49:52.680
It still doesn't do that well, but it does do it.

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:54.940
This is an important contrast to Ana,

00:49:54.940 --> 00:50:00.860
who has stepped far more dramatically outside her comfort zone and found that she is, for the most part,

00:50:00.940 --> 00:50:07.180
overwhelmed by the scope and intensity of what Christian is asking, far more than any small part.

00:50:07.180 --> 00:50:13.180
And again, it needs to be highlighted that Christian really isn't presented as breaking through any significant hurdles

00:50:13.250 --> 00:50:17.470
There is no conflict leading up to Christian asking Ana out for coffee

00:50:17.660 --> 00:50:24.440
There's no panic attack about wanting to take a romantic interest flying in his helicopter. It's effortless.

00:50:24.440 --> 00:50:31.380
There's a lot of nuanced argument over the idea of 'show don't tell'; like we can come up with all kinds of examples

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:38.420
where just telling the audience what's going on is actually pretty effective, and then we could get into further debate over whether or not those examples

00:50:38.420 --> 00:50:44.300
are still supported, or themselves support visual or environmental evidence that shows the thing we're being told,

00:50:44.300 --> 00:50:46.540
and thus it's all a combination of showing and telling...

00:50:46.540 --> 00:50:47.900
But this

00:50:47.900 --> 00:50:49.640
right here

00:50:49.640 --> 00:50:56.589
Christian Grey just *saying* it's a big deal that he's never had in his sleeping bed instead of his sex bed

00:50:57.080 --> 00:51:02.100
This right here is the poster child for the kind of limp storytelling

00:51:02.100 --> 00:51:08.980
that leads to getting your writing back from the editor with 'show don't tell' written in big red letters in the margin.

00:51:08.980 --> 00:51:11.600
We are told that this is a conflict;

00:51:11.680 --> 00:51:14.920
that this is all outside Christian's comfort zone

00:51:14.920 --> 00:51:20.420
but that conflict is supported by absolutely nothing else

00:51:20.880 --> 00:51:28.360
As a result, this point ends up falling into a hazy space where it's not a satisfying parallel between the two characters

00:51:28.360 --> 00:51:30.680
because the stakes are so disproportionate,

00:51:30.680 --> 00:51:37.700
but it's also not just cute banter, because there's clearly supposed to be weight to it in the cinematic language.

00:51:37.940 --> 00:51:42.900
This is the culmination of a scene where Christian tells Ana the first inkling of his dark past

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:46.600
and admits that he's still friends with the woman who raped him when he was a teenager

00:51:46.609 --> 00:51:51.788
The characters are framed at a path's dead end on the edge of dark water

00:51:51.788 --> 00:52:00.300
Ana is trapped visually in the noisy chaotic half of the frame, with Christian acting as the barrier between a cage and freedom.

00:52:00.300 --> 00:52:03.040
The soundtrack is tense, kind of sinister;

00:52:03.200 --> 00:52:07.640
Visually, the color palette of the scene gets colder as the scene progresses;

00:52:07.640 --> 00:52:10.240
When they walk down the path, the highlights are warm

00:52:10.240 --> 00:52:11.600
at the docks, they're neutral

00:52:11.600 --> 00:52:14.500
and when we come in for the close-up, 
they're cool and muted

00:52:14.580 --> 00:52:19.260
The scene is emotionally and literally getting darker.

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:25.720
But for what? This is the kind of moment that encapsulates why this movie stuck with me in the way it did.

00:52:25.880 --> 00:52:32.540
Moments of talented, subtle filmmaking, in the service of material that just does it no justice.

00:52:32.540 --> 00:52:35.200
The biggest example of this is the negotiation.

00:52:35.450 --> 00:52:38.049
Earlier, we discussed how the middle of the book is a mess.

00:52:38.080 --> 00:52:43.059
Lots of disjointed chapters, repeated scenes, fragmented developments, and awful stuff.

00:52:43.140 --> 00:52:45.740
This is where they managed to sort most of that out.

00:52:45.800 --> 00:52:50.739
The scene is an amalgamation of mostly chapters 13 and 15: two of the worst in the book.

00:52:50.740 --> 00:52:52.980
I say 'worst', because they are a couple of the scenes

00:52:52.980 --> 00:53:00.160
where we get the clearest example of Christian just absolutely disregarding Ana's agency, comfort, and explicit wishes.

00:53:00.167 --> 00:53:06.120
In the first, a dinner meeting: Ana explicitly asks that they stay in public for the conversation

00:53:06.300 --> 00:53:09.559
and Christian immediately moves her to a private dining room.

00:53:09.800 --> 00:53:24.540
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:53:25.000 --> 00:53:30.820
In the second, the actual meat and potatoes negotiation: Christian keeps pressing Ana to drink more

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:32.880
despite nna being a lightweight.

00:53:33.000 --> 00:54:19.760
[reading aloud from the novel]

00:54:20.040 --> 00:54:25.920
[distorted voice]

00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:27.680
Mmmm...Yeah!

00:54:27.680 --> 00:54:32.640
Anyway, the filmmakers have pulled all of the negotiations stuff together into a single scene

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:40.160
that really gives us an idea of what things maybe could have looked like in an alternate universe where Mitchell had less control over the project.

00:54:40.340 --> 00:54:42.760
It's witty. It's sassy. It's fun.

00:54:42.760 --> 00:54:45.020
There's a cute power play between the characters...

00:54:45.020 --> 00:54:49.580
This is part of the overall trend of imbuing Movie!Ana with more agency and awareness

00:54:49.580 --> 00:54:54.220
She isn't getting under his skin by accident. 
She's deliberately flirting...

00:54:54.300 --> 00:54:56.400
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Find 'anal fisting'."

00:54:56.580 --> 00:54:57.940
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"I'm all ears..."

00:54:58.200 --> 00:54:59.620
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Strike it out."

00:55:04.460 --> 00:55:06.460
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Strike out vaginal fisting too"

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:07.860
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"You sure?"

00:55:07.860 --> 00:55:09.620
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Yeah."

00:55:09.620 --> 00:55:12.100
It also ties into the changes to Christian.

00:55:12.110 --> 00:55:16.999
Ana isn't afraid of what he's going to do;
how he's going to respond

00:55:17.400 --> 00:55:20.269
She's not worried that she's making him angry

00:55:20.269 --> 00:55:22.980
It creates a much needed dramatic moment

00:55:22.980 --> 00:55:27.760
where it looks like things might actually work out; that they'll be able to reach a proper compromise

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:29.900
before everything starts to fall apart again.

00:55:29.900 --> 00:55:35.200
This is the midpoint: the false high; and for the most part this scene works in its own little bubble

00:55:35.200 --> 00:55:40.300
I mean, out of the bubble, it's a complete waste because despite the fact that they have this whole negotiation

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:43.560
Where Ana gets basically 
all the concessions that she wants

00:55:43.560 --> 00:55:46.980
and then in the next scene at her graduation, 
she tells Christian 'yes',

00:55:47.040 --> 00:55:49.740
she still doesn't physically sign the contract,

00:55:49.740 --> 00:55:53.820
and it becomes something that they argue about on-and-off for the rest of the book / movie

00:55:54.240 --> 00:55:57.800
because both do a really poor job at distinguishing between

00:55:57.900 --> 00:56:06.420
kinky sex (which is what they still do), and the full on 24/7 total package Dom/Sub lifestyle that Christian wants.

00:56:06.420 --> 00:56:09.700
And this is where the movie actually stumbles...

00:56:09.700 --> 00:56:13.840
Counter-intuitively: getting less coherent by being better.

00:56:13.840 --> 00:56:20.060
Like, okay: it still doesn't make sense in the book what the actual stakes are, because the two of them are just endlessly arguing about nothing

00:56:20.080 --> 00:56:25.920
But that kind of gets worse in the movie because the movie versions of the characters are at least nominally

00:56:25.940 --> 00:56:29.900
capable of having an honest and open conversation about their relationship

00:56:30.100 --> 00:56:36.880
So changing it, to be a scene where Ana is sober and professional and gets Christian to make numerous compromises,

00:56:36.880 --> 00:56:39.360
but never addresses the punishment angle,

00:56:39.540 --> 00:56:47.320
Actually makes less sense, than a scene where Christian gets Ana drunk and steamrolls her opinions with a few minor concessions.

00:56:47.360 --> 00:56:55.460
But still: the lighting works, the timing works, the acting works, Danny Elfman's score is at its most...Danny Elfman

00:57:00.440 --> 00:57:04.100
Eroticism is used to build the characters and tell the story,

00:57:04.100 --> 00:57:09.840
which is not what can be said for about 90% of the sex in both the book and the movie.

00:57:09.840 --> 00:57:11.840
I think the scenes in Fifty Shades are well shot.

00:57:12.000 --> 00:57:17.000
They're well lit. They're well scored. And they managed to convey an engaging level of eroticism

00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:19.760
so they're at least trashy fun...

00:57:19.760 --> 00:57:24.500
But there's a deeper problem where they fail to work as scenes.

00:57:24.500 --> 00:57:29.080
Almost all of them can be cut from the film and leave little impact with their absence.

00:57:29.080 --> 00:57:33.720
This actually makes a lot of sense, if we go all the way back and review the origins of the text.

00:57:33.720 --> 00:57:37.880
Master of the Universe, as you'll recall, was originally published on fanfiction.net

00:57:37.940 --> 00:57:44.920
which has a prohibition on sexually explicit content. How effective that prohibition has historically been is up for debate...

00:57:44.920 --> 00:57:50.060
But regardless, Erika Mitchell worked around it by posting saucy versions of the story there

00:57:50.060 --> 00:57:52.759
and more explicit versions to her personal website

00:57:52.760 --> 00:57:57.680
so if it seems like the characters do all their relevant character talking and then stop to have sex

00:57:57.680 --> 00:58:01.980
that these sex scenes are a post hoc addition to the actual story,

00:58:02.120 --> 00:58:03.740
It's because they are.

00:58:03.740 --> 00:58:07.140
This is all just a roundabout way of getting around to talking about the end of the film

00:58:07.280 --> 00:58:12.880
So, when the film was in production: the second and third films had not technically been greenlit yet

00:58:12.930 --> 00:58:17.809
There were contracts securing principal creatives in the event of the second and third films being made

00:58:17.849 --> 00:58:24.528
But Fifty Shades of Grey went into production with the possibility that it would be the only adaptation actually made

00:58:24.528 --> 00:58:31.700
I know it seems silly to think that Paramount would only make one of the books into a movie in a post Harry Potter / Hunger Games world

00:58:32.060 --> 00:58:39.480
But also consider that while Fifty Shades' rise to fame had been swift, there was always the possibility that it would fade just as quickly

00:58:39.480 --> 00:58:44.620
If you buy the film rights in 2013 for a book that had only really been out for a year and a half

00:58:44.660 --> 00:58:50.320
How sure are you that audiences will still 
be around in 4? 5 years?

00:58:50.320 --> 00:58:54.880
By the time Darker and Freed were greenlit, Grey was already deep into post-production

00:58:54.880 --> 00:59:00.320
I bring it up, because it places unique creative pressure on the director and screenwriter

00:59:00.320 --> 00:59:08.740
who now need to try and craft a movie that will work both as a potential standalone film, and as the first act of a three-part series

00:59:08.740 --> 00:59:13.860
With that as foreplay, let's talk about the final scene in the playroom: the belt whipping scene

00:59:13.940 --> 00:59:15.620
[sound of belt hitting]
[Ana huffs out a pained breath]

00:59:15.620 --> 00:59:20.800
Alright, so something vague has gone wrong with Christian's vague business stuff.

00:59:20.800 --> 00:59:27.340
In the book, I think it's that one of Christian's cargo boats with relief aid food bound for Africa is sunk by...

00:59:27.840 --> 00:59:30.360
pirates? or something?

00:59:30.900 --> 00:59:34.220
And he's sunk into a deep funk over this

00:59:34.220 --> 00:59:38.280
Ana tries to sex him back to happiness, by letting him tie her up and blindfold her

00:59:38.280 --> 00:59:41.020
but it doesn't work, because it's not extreme enough

00:59:41.029 --> 00:59:43.819
So he's sitting up late at night playing sad piano.

00:59:43.820 --> 00:59:50.640
The two of them have a very confusing argument about the contract that Ana has agreed to, but not signed,

00:59:50.640 --> 00:59:56.780
that ultimately kind of revolves around the fact that Ana really just doesn't like the idea of punishment.

00:59:56.780 --> 01:00:00.880
This is actually where a lot of kink and BDSM enthusiasts take the most umbrage

01:00:00.880 --> 01:00:05.680
because of the specific dominant/submissive interaction of rules and punishments and rewards

01:00:05.680 --> 01:00:10.720
is where Erika Mitchell basically says that if people enjoy *those* kinds of games,

01:00:10.760 --> 01:00:13.860
it's because they're emotionally damaged

01:00:14.140 --> 01:00:15.580
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Why?"

01:00:17.500 --> 01:00:18.960
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"It's the way I am."

01:00:19.080 --> 01:00:25.160
Either way, the conversation gets more confusing when Christian insists that the contract is still in force in spirit,

01:00:25.160 --> 01:00:29.200
even though she didn't sign it, and if she acts out there will be punishment.

01:00:29.200 --> 01:00:33.080
And so Ana says, 
'okay fine - show me how bad it can get'

01:00:33.080 --> 01:00:37.820
And Christian brings her into the playroom and tells her he's going to whip her with a belt 6 times.

01:00:37.820 --> 01:00:40.480
Now, I want to reiterate something I said earlier.

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:42.080
From a story standpoint,

01:00:42.080 --> 01:00:48.700
it's okay if Christian is a bit of a bastard, because it gives the characters something to conflict over

01:00:48.700 --> 01:00:50.280
Just...[sound of frustration]

01:00:50.280 --> 01:00:52.680
...something needs to come of it

01:00:52.800 --> 01:00:59.200
One of the things that Sam Taylor-Johnson wanted to do with this scene, was have Ana use her safe word 'red'

01:00:59.200 --> 01:01:01.560
while Christian keeps going.

01:01:01.620 --> 01:01:07.900
And that... is actually a brilliant change, or it would have been if it had happened.

01:01:07.940 --> 01:01:11.839
It properly centers the conflict around Christian's actual character flaws

01:01:11.840 --> 01:01:17.680
It calls attention to his selfishness. Makes his disregard for Anastasia's well-being explicit

01:01:17.680 --> 01:01:23.960
and gives Ana a very, very good reason to leave the relationship IMMEDIATELY.

01:01:23.960 --> 01:01:26.440
It is an overt betrayal of her trust

01:01:26.440 --> 01:01:32.520
It says that Christian thinks he's in control, but ultimately lacks the self-control to restrain himself

01:01:32.520 --> 01:01:39.820
It points out that his love of rules is ultimately a shallow facade to justify his very mundane abuse

01:01:39.960 --> 01:01:46.080
That right there *shows us* the thing that Mitchell is going to try to *tell us* in the next book.

01:01:46.080 --> 01:01:49.820
[reading aloud from the novel]

01:01:49.820 --> 01:01:52.560
It would have been a great change

01:01:52.840 --> 01:01:55.920
and Erica Mitchell lost her mind.

01:01:56.580 --> 01:02:01.520
Reports from set are that this dialogue change triggered an hour-long shouting match

01:02:01.520 --> 01:02:03.420
in front of cast and crew

01:02:03.420 --> 01:02:05.520
until ultimately Mitchell got her way.

01:02:05.520 --> 01:02:10.380
The scene as included in the movie plays out pretty much exactly the same as it does in the book;

01:02:10.380 --> 01:02:19.440
where Ana takes the whipping and then freaks out because she can't quite process that Christian likes the S &amp; M parts of BDSM

01:02:19.940 --> 01:02:24.620
Anyway, Sam Taylor-Johnson and Kelly Marcel are far better storytellers than Erica Mitchell

01:02:24.620 --> 01:02:27.740
and they understand Mitchell's story better than she does

01:02:27.760 --> 01:02:34.560
as evidenced by the closing moments of the film, where Ana goes to leave, Cristian moves to intercept her, and she tells him "no"

01:02:37.640 --> 01:02:40.100
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Stop. No!"

01:02:46.160 --> 01:02:50.460
Of course that would play a million times better if the story had actually been about boundaries

01:02:50.480 --> 01:02:58.458
But as it is, it still plays as part of an artistically strong final scene culminating in the fantastic final shot of the theatrical edition

01:03:00.860 --> 01:03:02.440
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"Ana"

01:03:02.440 --> 01:03:04.440
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Christian"

01:03:06.980 --> 01:03:15.100
The Unrated Edition adds a sad montage that is weaker as a standalone film, but ties into Fifty Shades Darker a bit more naturally

01:03:15.100 --> 01:03:18.620
Again, that challenge of making a film that will work as both.

01:03:18.720 --> 01:03:27.240
It's kind of the bottom line here: the film is surprisingly well made, with a deliberate artfulness for a movie that still, ultimately - isn't very good

01:03:27.240 --> 01:03:30.760
Its watchable, and it has its distinct highlights and craft and skill

01:03:30.760 --> 01:03:36.980
There are scenes and moments that are good, even great, but the whole is still less than the sum of its parts

01:03:37.140 --> 01:03:40.420
undercut by fundamentally flawed source material

01:03:40.720 --> 01:03:43.740
and with all that: conclusion of video

01:03:43.740 --> 01:03:45.920
blahdey, blah closing statements...

01:03:45.920 --> 01:03:49.120
We're going to keep going with this. That's right. We're not done!

01:03:49.120 --> 01:03:53.980
We've got two more of these suckers to get through and it's all downhill from here

01:03:53.989 --> 01:03:55.229
Oh, yeah!

01:03:55.229 --> 01:03:58.399
Just you wait: some stuff to look forward to when we talk about Darker:

01:03:58.560 --> 01:04:04.020
Sam Taylor-Johnson and Kelly Marcel getting fired! For, you know trying to make the movie better..

01:04:04.020 --> 01:04:10.640
The serial thickness ramping up to high gear. The budget cuts. The helicopter crash. The stalkers.

01:04:11.180 --> 01:04:13.380
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"Why do you have my bank account details?"

01:04:13.380 --> 01:04:17.160
[movie dialogue]
"Online hits don't automatically translate to print sales."

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:19.560
[movie dialogue - Ana]
Well... But they could."

01:04:19.640 --> 01:04:25.540
[movie dialogue - Christian]
"The right term is a sadist... I get off on punishing women; women who look like..."

01:04:25.540 --> 01:04:27.280
[movie dialogue - Ana]
"...like you mother."

01:04:29.340 --> 01:04:35.740
[movie dialogue]
"Our reporter, Rachel Taylor. Rachel..."
"Thank you John. I'm speaking to you live from the private helicopter terminal..."

01:04:36.660 --> 01:04:38.660
